    "report": "The clock change is committed on `main` as `6ceb86e`. I did not push. The full test suite passes (269 suites, 1656 tests), and typecheck and eslint are clean on the changed files.\n\n**What changed**\n\n- **Messaging:** `Conversation.start`, `send` and `report` now take their time as a required parameter, with no `new Date()` default. `ReportMessageCommandHandler` gets the clock, since it used to fall back to the machine's time. `messagingWiring` requires the clock instead of defaulting it.\n- **Notifications:** `NoticeWindow.admit`, `MessageNoticeOwing.owe` and `Notice.accept` take their time as required parameters. `MessageSentIntegrationEventHandler` and `NoticeDelivery` now receive the clock. `notificationsWiring` takes the window and the clock as required arguments.\n- **Composition root:** `createApplication` passes the same `clock` it already builds from `CLOCK_OFFSET_DAYS` into Notifications.\n\nNo API behaviour changed. The Messaging and Notifications e2e specs also still pass.\n\n**Decisions to review**\n\n- **Specs:** Many specs relied on the omitted default time. I gave them a fixed instant (`2026-03-01` for Messaging, `2027-05-01` for Notifications), and replaced the `new Date()` calls in `ConversationAttachments.spec.ts`. The assertions are unchanged.\n- **`messageNoticeWindowSeconds`:** I removed its default of 900 in `notificationsWiring`. This is configuration, not a time read, but `createApplication` always passes it, so nothing changes at runtime.\n- **Left alone:**\n  - The two e2e fixtures that read `Date.now()` (`messageClock.routes.e2e.spec.ts` and `calendar-conflicts.routes.e2e.spec.ts`). They compare against the real clock on purpose.\n  - The `new Date(each.sentAt)` calls in `Conversation.fromSnapshot`. They rebuild stored timestamps, not the current time.\n- **Branch:** `main` is the default branch, and the tool guidance says to branch first. You asked for the current branch, so I committed there. Say if you want it moved to a new branch.\n- **Server:** I did not start a server, so the port 4471 instruction didn't apply.",
